cipating in the academic research community through meaningful engagement with university faculty. cite25†Student programs Supporting the next generation of researchers through a wide range of programming. cite26†R&D programs Collaborating with industry and academic experts to support foundational science and innovation. cite27†Locations Find your place in our global offices and research labs. L70: \nL71: cite31†Blog L72: \nL73: About\nL74: ## Translating discovery into real-world impact\nL75: \nL76: cite28†People Our researchers drive advancements in computer science through both fundamental and applied research. cite29†Teams Collaborative groups tackling the world's most challenging AI problems. L77: \nL78: cite32†Google Research L79: \nL80: cite33†Google AI Learn about all our AI†ai.google cite34†Google DeepMind Explore the frontier of AI†deepmind.google cite35†Google Labs Try our AI experiments†labs.google L81: \nL82: [Button: Research ]\nL83: \nL84: [Button: Resources ]\nL85: \nL86: cite30†Conferences & events L87: [Button: Careers ]\nL88: \nL89: cite31†Blog L90: \nL91: [Button: About ]\nL92: \nL93: [Input: Search]\nL94: \nL95: Search\nL96: \nL97: cite36†Image: ​Sequential Attention: Making AI models leaner and faster without sacrificing accuracy†storage.googleapis.com L98: # ​Sequential Attention: Making AI models leaner and faster without sacrificing accuracy\nL99: \nL100: February 4, 2026\nL101: \nL102: Thomas Fu, Principal Engineer, and Kyriakos Axiotis, Senior Scientist, Google Research\nL103: \nL104: We introduce a subset selection algorithm for making large scale ML models more efficient.\nL105: ## Quick links\nL106: \nL107:   * cite37†Paper†arxiv.org L108: Feature selection is the process of identifying and retaining the most informative subset of input variables while discarding irrelevant or redundant noise. A fundamental challenge in both machine learning and deep learning, cite38†feature selection†www.geeksforgeeks.org is cite39†NP-hard†en.wikipedia.org (i.e., a problem that is mathematically \"impossible\" to solve perfectly and quickly for large groups of data), and as such, it remains a highly challenging area of research.\nL109: In modern deep neural networks, feature selection is further complicated by intricate non-linear feature interactions. A feature may appear statistically insignificant on its own but become critical when combined with others within the network's non-linear layers. Conversely, a feature’s contribution may appear significant in isolation, but made redundant when taking other features into account.\nL110: The core challenge lies in identifying essential features for retention while effectively pruning redundancy within complex model architectures.\nL111: More broadly, many ML optimization tasks can be cast as subset selection problems, of which feature selection is a special case. For example, embedding dimension tuning can be viewed as selecting a subset of embedding chunks, and weight pruning as selecting a subset of entries from the weight matrix. Therefore devising a general solution for the subset selection problem that is applicable to modern deep learning tasks can be highly impactful for building the most efficient models.\nL112: Today, we explore our solution to the subset selection problem, called cite37†Sequential Attention†arxiv.org . Sequential Attention uses a greedy selection mechanism to sequentially and adaptively select the best next component (like a layer, block, or feature) to add to the model.\nL113: While adaptive greedy algorithms are known to provide strong guarantees for various subset selection problems, such as cite40†submodular optimization†en.wikipedia.org , naïvely applying such algorithms would increase the training cost by many orders of magnitude. To tackle this scalability issue, we integrate selection directly into the model training process by performing selection within a single model training.\nL114: This ensures that Sequential Attention can be applied to large scale ML models with minimal overhead without sacrificing accuracy or complexity. Here we will analyze how Sequential Attention works and show how it’s being used in real-world scenarios to optimize the structure of deep learning models.\nL115: ## How Sequential Attention works\nL116: Sequential Attention leverages the weighting power of the attention mechanism to build a subset step-by-step. In contrast to standard \"one-shot\" attention, in which all candidates are weighted simultaneously, Sequential Attention addresses the cite41†NP-hard†en.wikipedia.org nature of subset selection by treating it as a sequential decision process.\nL117: This is particularly effective for identifying high-order non-linear interactions often missed by \"cite42†filter methods†www.geeksforgeeks.org ”, which provide the simplest way to pick a subset by focusing only on the merits of each individual item.\nL118: cite43†Image: SequentialAttention2_FeatureSelection†storage.googleapis.com L119: \nL120: Feature Selection in neural networks: Selective pruning of input features to optimize performance. By \"switching off\" low-utility features, the model simplifies the learning task and reduces the risk of overfitting.\nL121: The core idea is to maintain a set of selected candidates and use them as context to find the next most informative candidate to select.\nL122: This is achieved through two primary methods: greedy selection, which allows the model to make a locally optimal decision around which element to include at each step, and importance evaluation, which uses “attention scores” (numerical values indicating the importance or relevance of different input parts) to quantify the importance of every candidate in addition to the currently selected candidates.\nL123: Like the cite44†attention mechanism†www.geeksforgeeks.org , Sequential Attention uses cite45†softmax†en.wikipedia.org as an importance ranking of different components. Yet unlike the attention mechanism, it works sequentially as opposed to one shot, allowing the selection algorithm to adapt to previous selections — a crucial property for high-quality importance ranking.\nL124: ## Sequential Attention benefits\nL125: \nL126: The primary benefits of Sequential Attention are:\nL127:   * Efficiency and accuracy: By allowing parallel processing of the candidates (once the attention scores are calculated), they can be evaluated faster than in traditional sequential selection.\nL128:   * Interpretability: The attention scores themselves offer a powerful diagnostic tool. Researchers can inspect the attention scores to see exactly which parts of the input a model prioritized when making a specific decision or generating a specific token. This makes the model's internal reasoning more interpretable than that of a black-box model.\nL129:   * Scalability: The ability to efficiently handle a large number of candidates is crucial for large-scale feature selection for modern neural networks.\nL130: ## Sequential Attention in action\nL131: ### Feature selection\nL132: \nL133: The standard feature selection method, i.e., greedy selection, is computationally expensive, as it requires re-training or re-evaluating the model for every potential feature at every step. In “cite37†Sequential Attention for Feature Selection†arxiv.org ”, we sought to replace this costly method with a much cheaper proxy: the model’s internal attention weights.\nL134: At each step, the Sequential Attention algorithm calculates attention weights for all remaining, unselected features, and permanently adds the feature with the highest attention score (the one to which the model is \"paying the most attention\") to the subset. The algorithm then re-runs the selection process (the process of feeding input data through a neural network, layer by layer, from input to output, to generate a prediction) and re-calculates the attention weights for the remaining features.\nL135: This recalculation naturally reflects the marginal gain (how much a feature contributes to performance, given the features already selected), allowing the model to effectively identify and avoid adding redundant features.\nL136: cite46†Image: SequentialAttention4_Results†storage.googleapis.com L137: \nL138: Feature selection performance. Prediction accuracy of the proposed approach (orange) vs. baselines. Our method achieves competitive or leading results across proteomics, image, and activity recognition benchmarks, confirming its robustness.\nL139: The Sequential Attention algorithm achieved state-of-the-art results across cite37†several neural network benchmarks†arxiv.org . Notably, it drastically improved efficiency, enabling a fast, one-pass implementation of greedy selection without the need for expensive, explicit marginal gain calculations.\nL140: The study also demonstrated that when applied to a simple cite47†linear regression†en.wikipedia.org model, the Sequential Attention algorithm is mathematically equivalent to the established cite48†Orthogonal Matching Pursuit†angms.science (OMP) algorithm. This equivalence is critical because OMP comes with provable guarantees of reliability and performance.\nL141: ### Block sparsification\nL142: \nL143: Neural network cite49†pruning†en.wikipedia.org is essential for deploying large models efficiently because it reduces the model size by removing unnecessary weights. Prior research pursued two largely separate paths: differentiable pruning, which uses trainable parameters as proxies for importance, and combinatorial optimization, which uses algorithms to search for the best sparse structure.\nL144: \nL145: cite50†Image: SequentialAttention3_MatrixSparsification†storage.googleapis.com L146: Matrix block sparsification: Identifying and zeroing out non-essential parameter blocks to optimize memory and speed. Unlike unstructured pruning, block-based sparsity leverages hardware acceleration for superior inference performance.\nL147: In “cite51†SequentialAttention++ for Block Sparsification: Differentiable Pruning Meets Combinatorial Optimization†arxiv.org ”, we sought to unite these two approaches into a coherent framework for structured neural network pruning that removes entire blocks or channels of weights to achieve real-world improvements on hardware accelerators like GPUs and TPUs.\nL148: The resulting algorithm, SequentialAttention++, provides a new way to discover the most important blocks of weight matrices, and shows significant gains in model compression and efficiency without sacrificing accuracy in ML tasks, e.g., cite52†ImageNet†www.image-net.org classification.\nL149: ## The future of sequential attention\nL150: As the increasing integration of AI models in science, engineering and business makes model efficiency more relevant than ever, model structure optimization is crucial for building highly effective yet efficient models. We have identified subset selection as a fundamental challenge related to model efficiency across various deep learning optimization tasks, and Sequential Attention has emerged as a pivotal technique for addressing these problems.\nL151: Moving forward, we aim to extend the applications of subset selection to increasingly complex domains.\nL152: ### Feature engineering with real constraints\nL153: Sequential Attention has demonstrated significant quality gains and efficiency savings in optimizing the feature embedding layer in large embedding models (LEMs) used in recommender systems. These models typically have a large number of heterogeneous features with large embedding tables, and so the tasks of feature selection/pruning, feature cross search and embedding dimension optimization are highly impactful.\nL154: In the future, we would like to allow these feature engineering tasks to take real inference constraints into account, enabling fully automated, continual feature engineering.\nL155: ### Large language model (LLM) pruning\nL156: \nL157: The SequentialAttention++ paradigm is a promising direction for LLM pruning. By applying this framework we can enforce structured sparsity (e.g., block sparsity), prune redundant attention heads, embedding dimensions or entire transformer blocks, and significantly reduce model footprint and inference latency while preserving predictive performance.\nL158: ### Drug discovery and genomics\nL159: \nL160: Feature selection is vital in the biological sciences. Sequential Attention can be adapted to efficiently extract influential genetic or chemical features from high-dimensional datasets, enhancing both the interpretability and accuracy of models in drug discovery and personalized medicine.\nL161: Current research focuses on scaling Sequential Attention to handle massive datasets and highly complex architectures more efficiently. Furthermore, ongoing efforts seek to identify superior pruned model structures and extend rigorous mathematical guarantees to real-world deep learning applications, solidifying the framework’s reliability across industries.\nL162: Subset selection is a core problem central to multiple optimization tasks in deep learning, while Sequential Attention is a key technique to solve these problems. In the future, we will explore more applications of subset selection to solve more challenging problems in broader domains\nL163: ## Conclusion\nL164: \nL165: Sequential Attention is an effective technique for multiple large-scale subset selection problems in deep learning and plays a key role in model architecture optimization. As these techniques evolve, they will solidify the future of machine learning, guaranteeing that powerful AI remains both accurate and accessible for years to come.\nL166: ## Acknowledgements\nL167: \nL168: We would like to express our gratitude to our research collaborators, Taisuke Yasuda, Lin Chen, Matthew Fahrbach, MohammadHossein Bateni, and Vahab Mirrokni, whose efforts have advanced the development of Sequential Attention. This work builds upon fundamental research in differentiable subset selection and combinatorial optimization to create more efficient and accessible AI models.\nL169: \nL170:   * Labels:\nL171:   * cite53†Algorithms & Theory L172: ## Quick links\nL173: \nL174:   * cite37†Paper†arxiv.org L175: \nL176: × ❮ ❯\nL177: \nL178: cite43†Image: SequentialAttention2_FeatureSelection†storage.googleapis.com L179: \nL180: cite50†Image: SequentialAttention3_MatrixSparsification†storage.googleapis.com L181: \nL182: cite54†Image: SequentialAttention4_Results†storage.googleapis.com L183: \nL184: ×\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nNew Released - Overview - Z.AI DEVELOPER DOCUMENT (https://docs.z.ai/release-notes/new-released)\nciteturn25986view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://docs.z.ai/release-notes/new-released\",\"lineno\":108}); Total lines: 165\nL0: > ## Documentation Index\nL1: >\nL2: > Fetch the complete documentation index at: cite0†/llms.txt L3: >\nL4: > Use this file to discover all available pages before exploring further.\nL5: \nL6: cite1†Skip to main content L7: \nL8: cite2†Overview - Z.AI DEVELOPER DOCUMENT home page†z.ai L9: \nL10: Search...\nL11: \nL12: Navigation\nL13: \nL14: New Released\nL15: \nL16: cite3†Guides cite4†API Reference cite5†Coding Plan cite6†Released Notes cite7†Terms and Policy cite8†Help Center L17: \nL18: # New Released\nL19: \nL20: Follow along with updates across Z.AI’s models\nL21: \nL22: Copy page Copy page\nL23: ##\nL24: \nL25: cite9†​ L26: \nL27: Models\nL28: \nL29: cite10†​ L30: \nL31: [Button: 2026-08-26]\nL32: \nL33: GLM-5.3-Flash\nL34:   * Native visual capabilities enable the model to observe interfaces, rendering results, and interaction feedback—creating a closed loop across code, browsers, and GUIs.\nL35:   * Efficient hybrid architecture: Combines linear and sparse attention with 320B total parameters and 18B activated, significantly reducing compute and KV-cache requirements.\nL36:   * Beyond coding: Supports office document and financial research workflows, autonomously breaking down goals, using tools, and refining outputs. Learn more in our cite11†documentation .*\nL37: cite12†​ L38: \nL39: [Button: 2026-08-18]\nL40: \nL41: GLM-5.3\nL42:   * Stronger Coding Capabilities: GLM-5.3 delivers a significant improvement in coding capabilities, achieving a 50% gain over GLM-5.2 on Z.ai Code Bench and reaching state-of-the-art (SOTA) performance among open-source models on public benchmarks, including Terminal Bench 3.0.\nL43:   * Emergent Cybersecurity Capabilities: GLM-5.3 matches Mythos 5 in white-box code review and vulnerability discovery. In collaboration with multiple cybersecurity teams, it has been tested on real-world targets and has identified a total of 2,436 vulnerabilities, including 1,097 medium- and high-severity vulnerabilities.Learn more in our cite13†documentation .*\nL44: cite14†​ L45: \nL46: [Button: 2026-06-16]\nL47: \nL48: GLM-5.2\nL49:   * Supports 1M lossless context, significantly improving long-horizon task capabilities and reducing context drift and goal forgetting in complex tasks\nL50:   * Achieves open-source SOTA performance on coding and long-horizon task benchmarks, delivering more stable results in complex system engineering and deep debugging\nL51:   * Significantly improves the real-world developer experience, with more reliable project-level context handling, adherence to engineering standards, and multi-platform development. Learn more in our cite15†documentation .*\nL52: cite16†​ L53: \nL54: [Button: 2026-04-07]\nL55: \nL56: GLM-5.1\nL57:   * Designed for long-horizon tasks, GLM-5.1 can work independently for up to 8 hours in a single run, enabling a full loop from planning and execution to iterative refinement and final delivery.\nL58:   * It demonstrates stronger engineering intelligence across autonomous planning, sustained execution, bug fixing, and strategy iteration, while achieving comprehensive capability alignment with Claude Opus 4.6. Built with multi-turn SFT, RL, and a process-quality evaluation framework, GLM-5.1 further improves stability, consistency, and tool use over extended tasks. Learn more in our cite17†documentation .*\nL59: cite18†​ L60: \nL61: [Button: 2026-02-12]\nL62: \nL63: GLM-5\nL64:   * Designed for complex system engineering and long-range Agent tasks, GLM-5 shifts the paradigm from coding to engineering, demonstrating strong deep-reasoning performance in backend architecture, complex algorithms, and stubborn bug fixing.\nL65:   * It directly benchmarks against Claude Opus 4.5 in code-logic density and systems-engineering capability, and integrates DeepSeek Sparse Attention for higher token efficiency while preserving long-context quality.Learn more in our cite19†documentation .*\nL66: \nL67: cite20†​ L68: [Button: 2026-02-03]\nL69: \nL70: GLM-OCR\nL71:   * We’ve launched GLM-OCR, a compact and high-performance optical character recognition model powered by the self-developed CogViT and GLM-0.5B encoder-decoder architecture, enabling efficient cross-modal alignment through its dedicated connection layer.\nL72:   * The update leverages CLIP pre-training on billions of image-text pairs to deliver robust visual semantic understanding and key token extraction capabilities, while maintaining a lightweight design for fast inference. Learn more in our cite21†documentation .*\nL73: cite22†​ L74: \nL75: [Button: 2026-01-19]\nL76: \nL77: GLM-4.7-Flash\nL78:   * We’ve launched GLM-4.7-Flash, a lightweight and efficient model designed as the free-tier version of GLM-4.7, delivering strong performance across coding, reasoning, and generative tasks with low latency and high throughput.\nL79:   * The update brings competitive coding capabilities at its scale, offering best-in-class general abilities in writing, translation, long-form content, role play, and aesthetic outputs for high-frequency and real-time use cases. Learn more in our cite23†documentation .*\nL80: \nL81: cite24†​ L82: [Button: 2026-01-14]\nL83: \nL84: GLM-Image\nL85:   * We’ve launched GLM-Image, a state-of-the-art image generation model built on a multimodal architecture and fully trained on domestic chips, combining autoregressive semantic understanding with diffusion-based decoding to deliver high-quality, controllable visual generation.\nL86:   * The update significantly enhances performance in knowledge-intensive scenarios, with more stable and accurate text rendering inside images, making GLM-Image especially well suited for commercial design, educational illustrations, and content-rich visual applications.Learn more in our cite25†documentation .*\nL87: cite26†​ L88: \nL89: [Button: 2025-12-22]\nL90: \nL91: GLM-4.7\nL92:   * We’ve released GLM-4.7, our foundation model with significant improvements in coding, reasoning, and agentic capabilities. It delivers more reliable code generation, stronger long-context understanding, and improved end-to-end task execution across real-world development workflows.\nL93:   * The update brings open-source SOTA performance on major coding and reasoning benchmarks, enhanced agentic coding for goal-driven, multi-step tasks, and improved front-end and document generation quality. Learn more in our cite23†documentation .*\nL94: cite27†​ L95: \nL96: [Button: 2025-12-11]\nL97: \nL98: AutoGLM-Phone-Multilingual\nL99:   * We’ve launched AutoGLM-Phone-Multilingual, our latest multimodal mobile automation framework that understands screen content and executes real actions through ADB. It enables natural-language task execution across 50+ mainstream apps, delivering true end-to-end mobile control.\nL100:   * The update introduces multilingual support (English & Chinese), enhanced workflow planning capabilities, and improved task execution reliability. Learn more in our cite28†documentation .*\nL101: \nL102: cite29†​ L103: \nL104: [Button: 2025-12-10]\nL105: GLM-ASR-2512\nL106: \nL107:   * We’ve launched GLM-ASR-2512, our ASR model, delivering industry-leading accuracy with a Character Error Rate of just 0.0717, and significantly improved performance across real-world multilingual and accent-rich scenarios.\nL108:   * The update introduces enhanced custom dictionary support and expanded specialized terminology recognition. Learn more in our cite30†documentation .*\nL109: \nL110: cite31†​ L111: \nL112: [Button: 2025-12-08]\nL113: \nL114: GLM-4.6V\nL115:   * We’re excited to introduce GLM-4.6V, Z.ai’s latest iteration in multimodal large language models. This version enhances vision understanding, achieving state-of-the-art performance in tasks involving images and text.\nL116:   * The update also expands the context window to 128K, enabling more efficient processing of long inputs and complex multimodal tasks. Learn more in our cite32†documentation .*\nL117: \nL118: cite33†​ L119: \nL120: [Button: 2025-09-30]\nL121: \nL122: GLM-4.6\nL123:   * We’ve launched GLM-4.6, the flagship coding model, showcasing enhanced performance in both public benchmarks and real-world programming tasks, making it the leading coding model in China.\nL124:   * The update also expands the context window to 200K, improving its ability to handle longer code and complex agent tasks. Learn more in our cite34†documentation .*\nL125: \nL126: cite35†​ L127: \nL128: [Button: 2025-08-11]\nL129: \nL130: GLM-4.5V\nL131:   * We’ve launched GLM-4.5V, a 100B-scale open-source vision reasoning model, supporting a broad range of visual tasks including video understanding, visual grounding, GUI agents and etc.\nL132:   * The update also adds a new thinking mode. Learn more in our cite36†documentation .*\nL133: \nL134: cite37†​ L135: \nL136: [Button: 2025-08-08]\nL137: \nL138: GLM Slide/Poster Agent(beta)\nL139:   * We’ve launched GLM Slide/Poster Agent, an AI-powered creation agent that combines information retrieval, content structuring, and visual layout design to generate professional-grade slides and posters from natural language instructions.\nL140:   * The update also brings a seamless integration of content generation with design conventions. Learn more in our cite38†documentation .*\nL141: \nL142: cite39†​ L143: \nL144: [Button: 2025-07-28]\nL145: \nL146: GLM-4.5 Series\nL147:   * We’ve launched GLM-4.5, our latest native agentic LLM, delivering doubled parameter efficiency and strong reasoning, coding, and agentic capabilities.\nL148:   * It also offers seamless one-click compatibility with the Claude Code framework. Learn more in our cite40†documentation .*\nL149: \nL150: cite41†​ L151: \nL152: [Button: 2025-07-15]\nL153: \nL154: CogVideoX-3\nL155:   * We’ve launched CogVideoX-3, an incremental upgrade to our video generation model with improved quality and new features.\nL156:   * It adds support for start and end frame synthesis. Learn more in our cite42†documentation .*\nL157: \nL158: Was this page helpful?\nL159: \nL160: Yes No\nL161: \nL162: cite43†x†x.com cite44†github†github.com cite45†discord†discord.gg cite46†linkedin†www.linkedin.com L163: \nL164: cite47†Powered byThis documentation is built and hosted on Mintlify, a developer documentation platform†www.mintlify.com --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMiniMax Agent Tech Blog (https://agent.minimax.io/docs/techblog)\nciteturn25986view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://agent.minimax.io/docs/techblog\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 15\nL0: > ## Documentation Index\nL1: >\nL2: > Fetch the complete documentation index at: cite0†/docs/llms.txt L3: >\nL4: > Use this file to discover all available pages before exploring further.\nL5: \nL6: cit